<body>
\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>An <strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong> defines rules used to permit or deny traffic passing through a network device according to matching criteria.</p>
            
            <h3>Matching Criteria</h3>
            <p>Depending on the type of ACL and the vendor, rules can filter traffic based on:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Source IP address</li>
                <li>Destination IP address</li>
                <li>Protocol (e.g., TCP, UDP, ICMP)</li>
                <li>Source / Destination <a href="common-network-ports.html">ports</a></li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Standard vs Extended ACLs</h3>
            <p><em>Note: This is vendor/platform terminology, heavily used in Cisco environments.</em></p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Standard ACLs:</strong> Typically filter traffic based <em>only</em> on the source IP address.</li>
                <li><strong>Extended ACLs:</strong> Can filter based on source IP, destination IP, protocol, and ports, offering granular control.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Rule Processing and Implicit Deny</h3>
            <p>ACLs are processed top-down in <strong>first-match</strong> behavior. Once a packet matches a rule, the action (permit or deny) is applied, and no further rules are checked.</p>
            <p>Crucially, almost all ACLs end with an invisible <strong>implicit deny</strong> rule. If a packet does not match any explicitly configured rule in the list, it is dropped.</p>
            
            <h3>Conceptual Example Policy</h3>
            <pre><code>Rule 1: Permit TCP from 10.10.10.0/24 to 10.20.20.50 eq 443
Rule 2: Deny IP any any (Implicit or explicit)</code></pre>
            <p>In this conceptual example, hosts on the <code>10.10.10.0/24</code> subnet can access a secure web server at <code>10.20.20.50</code>. All other traffic is denied. Note that syntax is not universal across vendors; always consult your platform's documentation.</p>
        
        </div>
        
